# Without the trailing slash as well: a worktree that shares the main
# checkout's dependencies does it with a *symlink*, and `node_modules/` matches
# only a directory — so the link showed up as an untracked file in every
# `git status` and was one `git add -A` away from being committed.
node_modules
node_modules/
out/
dist/
release/
*.log
.DS_Store
.vite/
resources/config/*.local.*

# TypeScript incremental build state: a local cache, and it changes on every
# build. Tracking it means every run shows a diff nobody wrote.
*.tsbuildinfo
# What a development run used to leave inside the checkout.
#
# The client keeps its files under the platform's own user directory now
# (`~/.config/mudengine` and friends -- see `src/main/app/home.ts`), so nothing
# here should reappear. These stay because a checkout that predates the move
# still has them and one `git add -A` away is somebody's password in a commit.
resources/config/user.yaml
resources/config/user.yaml.*
resources/config/profiles/
resources/config/realms/
resources/config/mob-lore.json*
resources/config/memory/
resources/config/fights/

# Session recordings. `logging.capture` is on by default (see
# `resources/config/default.yaml`), so these accumulate from ordinary play.
#
# They land in <userData>/logs unless `logging.directory` says otherwise --
# and it may well point somewhere in this tree, which is why these are matched
# by name anywhere rather than at one path. They hold everything the server
# sent and every command sent back, which on a manual login includes a
# password: `npm run check:secrets` exists for exactly this, and these must
# never be committed.
*.mudcap.jsonl
logs/

# MegaMUD itself: 27MB of somebody else's program, its help file and 4,490
# recorded paths. Read from (docs/mega-paramud.md) and converted by
# `npm run build:loops`; the converted loops are committed, this is not.
mega-paramud/
captures/
# Claude Code's own working tree: agents, skills and worktrees it writes for
# itself. It has never been committed and must not be -- `settings.local.json`
# under it holds machine-specific permissions, and the rest is a cache of this
# machine's assistant configuration, not of the project.
.claude/

# Prose is kept locally, not in the repository. CLAUDE.md, TODO.md, CHANGELOG.md
# and everything under docs/ are working notes that change on almost every
# session; tracking them meant every commit carried a diff of the notes taken
# while making it. They still exist on disk -- `git rm --cached` took them out
# of the index and left the files alone.
#
# `*.MD` is here because the case matters on Linux and does not on the machines
# these files came from.
*.md
*.MD

# Screenshots dropped in the root while looking at something. `image*.png`
# rather than `image-*.png`: the first one a paste produces is `image.png`,
# with no number, and the hyphenated form is exactly the pattern that misses it.
image*.png

# The todo queue is prose plus pasted screenshots, all of it local — the
# working notes rule above, applied to the one directory that exists to hold
# them. The `*.md` pattern already caught the words; the assets beside them
# (a paste renamed by hand, an archived screenshot named after its todo)
# escaped `image*.png` one filename at a time.
todo/
docs/

# The one exception, and it is deliberate: the root README is the front page
# of the repository, so it is the one piece of prose worth keeping under
# version control. Only the root one -- `/README.md`, anchored. It used to be
# `!**/README.md`, which pulled in `build/README.md` and, before `docs/` was
# ignored as a directory, `docs/greatermud/README.md`; those are working notes
# like everything else and were taken out of the index in the same change.
!/README.md

# Reference source cloned for comparison, never ours to track. `MMUD-Explorer`
# is a 138 MB VB6 tree with a realm database in it; it is read here and lives
# nowhere in this repository's history.
.scratch/
